    { id: "roenneberg2004", cite: "Roenneberg T, Kuehnle T, Pramstaller PP, Ricken J, Havel M, Guth A, Merrow M. A marker for the end of adolescence. Curr Biol. 2004;14(24):R1038-R1039. doi:10.1016/j.cub.2004.11.039", grade: "large population survey", found: "external", supports: "Chronotype gets progressively later through adolescence, reaches its latest point at around age 20, then becomes earlier again." },
    { id: "sargent2014", cite: "Sargent C, Lastella M, Halson SL, Roach GD. The impact of training schedules on the sleep and fatigue of elite athletes. Chronobiol Int. 2014;31(10):1160-1168. doi:10.3109/07420528.2014.957306", grade: "observational cohort (70 athletes, 7 sports)", found: "external (cited in Zotero items, e.g. Fullagar et al. 2016)", supports: "Nights before training days gave less sleep than nights before rest days; early morning starts reduce sleep and raise pre-training fatigue; suggests naps and sleep hygiene when early starts are unavoidable." },
    { id: "vitale2017", cite: "Vitale JA, Weydahl A. Chronotype, physical activity, and sport performance: a systematic review. Sports Med. 2017;47(9):1859-1868. doi:10.1007/s40279-017-0741-z", grade: "systematic review (10 studies)", found: "external", supports: "Morning types perform better and perceive less effort in the morning than evening types; recommends accounting for time of day and chronotype when scheduling; notes the topic is under-studied." },
    { id: "smith2013", cite: "Smith RS, Efron B, Mah CD, Malhotra A. The impact of circadian misalignment on athletic performance in professional football players. Sleep. 2013;36(12):1999-2001. doi:10.5665/sleep.3248", grade: "retrospective observational analysis (40 seasons of games)", found: "Zotero", supports: "West Coast NFL teams beat the point spread about twice as often in evening games against East Coast teams, consistent with playing nearer their body-clock peak." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Picture a 7 a.m. lift. Same plan, same weights, same coach. For one athlete it is mid-morning in their body. For the athlete next to them it is still the middle of the night.

That difference has a name: chronotype. It is the natural timing of someone's body clock, early or late. Today is about what the research actually says about it, and what it doesn't.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline(["Is 7 a.m. the ", { t: "same workout", color: "accent" }, " for every athlete?"], { y: 1.75, w: 10.4, h: 2.9, size: 80 });
        s.source("Chronotype and peak performance: Facer-Childs & Brandstaetter, Current Biology, 2015", { y: 5.9, w: 9, refs: ["facerchilds2015"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Here is why this matters for us in particular. Body clocks drift later through the teenage years, and a very large survey found they hit their latest point at around age twenty. Then they start moving earlier again. In other words, our athletes are at the age when the body clock runs latest.

At the same time, a lot of college sport happens early. A study of seventy elite athletes found that on nights before training days they slept less than before rest days, and early starts were the main reason. Shorter sleep went with more fatigue at the start of training.

So the latest body clocks are meeting the earliest schedules.`,
      build(s) {
        s.rect(8.89, 0, 4.443, 7.5, { fill: "soft" });
        s.headline("Our athletes are at the age when body clocks run latest.", { y: 1.35, w: 7.4, h: 2.4, size: 52 });
        s.lead("Body clocks drift later through the teens and reach their latest point around age 20.", { y: 4.05, w: 6.9, h: 1.3, size: 24 });
        s.source("Roenneberg et al., Current Biology, 2004", { y: 5.9, w: 6.5, refs: ["roenneberg2004"] });
        s.text(9.45, 1.45, 3.2, 2.7, "Early sessions cut the sleep before them.", { font: "condBold", size: 34, lineH: 1.05 });
        s.text(9.45, 3.75, 3.15, 1.9, "Less sleep before training days, and more fatigue at the start.", { font: "light", size: 20, color: "muted", lineH: 1.2 });
        s.source("Sargent et al., Chronobiol Int, 2014", { x: 9.45, y: 5.9, w: 3.15, refs: ["sargent2014"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 75,
      notes: `This is the key study, and it is small, so I will be straight about that: twenty field hockey players, tested at six times across the day.

Early types peaked around noon. Late types peaked around eight in the evening. The players in the middle peaked around four. And the late types swung the most: their performance varied by about a quarter between their worst and best time of day.

Look where the 7 a.m. line falls. The early type is climbing toward their best. The late type is near the bottom of their day. Same session, different body clock.

A larger review agrees with the direction, morning types do better in the morning, but says the topic is still under-studied.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Same session, different body clock.", { y: 1.2, w: 9, h: 0.8, size: 40 });
        const x0 = 1.25, x1 = 12.25, yb = 5.45, H = 2.85;
        const X = hr => x0 + (hr - 6) / 16 * (x1 - x0);
        const curve = (peak, amp, base) => {
          const pts = [];
          for (let hr = 6; hr <= 22; hr += 0.5) {
            const d = (hr - peak) / 5.2;
            pts.push([X(hr), yb - (base + amp * Math.exp(-d * d))]);
          }
          return pts;
        };
        s.line(x0, yb, x1, yb, { color: "muted", w: 1.25 });
        s.line(X(7), 2.35, X(7), yb, { color: "muted", w: 1.25, dash: "dash" });
        s.text(X(7) + 0.12, 2.25, 2.4, 0.4, "A 7 a.m. session", { font: "cond", size: 18, color: "ink" });
        s.path(curve(12.2, 1.55, 0.95), { smooth: true, color: "muted", w: 3.5 });
        s.path(curve(19.7, 2.55, 0.12), { smooth: true, color: "accent", w: 5 });
        s.text(X(12.2) - 1.6, yb - 2.95, 3.2, 0.4, "Early type: peak near noon", { font: "cond", size: 20, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        s.text(X(19.7) - 2.6, yb - 3.2, 3.4, 0.4, "Late type: peak near 8 p.m.", { font: "condBold", size: 20, color: "accent", align: "right" });
        [[6, "6 a.m."], [10, "10 a.m."], [14, "2 p.m."], [18, "6 p.m."], [22, "10 p.m."]].forEach(([hr, t]) => {
          s.line(X(hr), yb, X(hr), yb + 0.1, { color: "muted", w: 1 });
          s.text(X(hr) - 0.7, yb + 0.15, 1.4, 0.36, t, { font: "cond", size: 15, color: "muted", align: "center" });
        });
        s.text(x0, yb + 0.6, 5.5, 0.36, "Illustrative shape; peaks at the reported times", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted" });
        s.source("Facer-Childs & Brandstaetter 2015 (20 field hockey players) · Vitale & Weydahl 2017", { x: 5.6, y: 6.05, w: 6.98, align: "right", refs: ["facerchilds2015", "vitale2017"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `So what do we do with this? We are not going to rebuild the calendar around every athlete's body clock. Classes, facilities and travel decide a lot. But three things are in our hands.

When an early session is fixed, guard the night before. The same study of elite athletes recommended earlier lights-out, good sleep habits, and a nap later in the day.

When we test or compare numbers, do it at the same time of day every time. A late type tested at 7 a.m. one month and 4 p.m. the next can look like they changed when they didn't.

And when we have a real choice, put the hardest work at midday or later rather than at dawn.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Put the clock into the plan.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.85, size: 44 });
        s.label("When", { x: 0.75, y: 2.35, w: 3 });
        s.label("Do", { x: 5.4, y: 2.35, w: 3 });
        const rows = [
          ["An early start is fixed", "Guard the night before."],
          ["We test or compare", "Same time of day, every time."],
          ["We have a choice", "Hard work at midday or later."],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([w, d], i) => {
          const y = 2.8 + i * 1.12;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.27, 4.4, 0.55, w, { font: "light", size: 24, color: "muted" });
          s.text(5.4, y + 0.22, 7.1, 0.6, d, { font: "medium", size: 28, lineH: 1.05 });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 6.16, 12.58, 6.16, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.cite("sargent2014", "facerchilds2015");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `One line to take away: count the clock. When a session happens is part of what it costs.

A 7 a.m. session asks more of a late-type athlete than of an early type, and our athletes are at the age when late types are most common.

Question for the room: which of our early sessions are early by choice, and which are early by habit? Some will have good reasons. Some may just be the way it has always been done.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Count the clock.", { y: 1.3, w: 11.5, h: 1.8, size: 104 });
        s.lead("When a session happens is part of what it costs the athlete.", { y: 3.4, w: 9.2, h: 0.9, size: 26 });
        s.line(0.75, 4.85, 1.75, 4.85, { color: "accent", w: 3 });
        s.text(0.75, 5.05, 11.6, 1.0, "Which of our early sessions are early by choice, and which by habit?", { font: "medium", size: 28, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15 });
      },
    },
  ],
};

