        s.label("What gets claimed", { x: 0.75, y: 1.7, w: 5 });
        s.text(0.75, 2.2, 5.4, 3.0, "Breathe this way to sleep better, get sick less and perform better.", { font: "light", size: 36, lineH: 1.12, color: "muted" });
        s.line(6.62, 1.75, 6.62, 5.6, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.label("What’s been measured best", { x: 7.1, y: 1.7, w: 5.4 });
        s.text(7.1, 2.2, 5.45, 3.0, "Slow breathing calms your heart rhythm while you do it.", { font: "condBold", size: 44, lineH: 1.05, color: "accent" });
        s.source("Laborde et al., Neurosci Biobehav Rev, 2022 (223 studies)", { x: 7.1, y: 5.9, w: 5.45, refs: ["laborde2022"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `The evidence is real, but it's narrow. Three problems.

First, most trials are small and short, and very few are in athletes. The best meta-analysis on stress pooled twelve trials, under eight hundred adults in total, mostly at moderate risk of bias. Its own authors warned about hype getting ahead of evidence.

Second, very different techniques get sold as one thing. Slow breathing, breath holds and rapid over-breathing do different things to the body.

Third, safety. Rapid over-breathing and long breath holds before swimming underwater have caused blackouts and drownings. None of that belongs in or near a pool.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The evidence is real, but narrow.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const rows = [
          ["Most trials are small, short and not in athletes.", "Fincham et al. 2023"],
          ["Very different techniques get sold as one thing.", "Balban et al. 2023"],
          ["Over-breathing before swimming underwater has caused drownings.", "Boyd et al. 2015"],
        ];
        rows.forEach(([t, src], i) => {
          const y = 2.6 + i * 1.22;
          s.line(0.75, y, 12.58, y, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
          s.text(0.75, y + 0.2, 9.4, 0.95, t, { font: "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.1, color: i === 2 ? "accent" : "ink" });
          s.source(src, { x: 10.2, y: y + 0.3, w: 2.38, h: 0.45, size: 14, align: "right", valign: "top" });
        });
        s.line(0.75, 6.26, 12.58, 6.26, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.cite("fincham2023", "balban2023", "boyd2015");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 75,
      notes: `Here are five claims you will hear, ranked by how much evidence sits behind them. The length of each line is our reading of the evidence, not a score.

At the top, slow breathing calming heart rhythm: over two hundred studies. Next, breathwork lowering stress: twelve trials, a small-to-medium effect. Then two single trials: slow breathing before bed improving how people rated their sleep, and cyclic sighing, long double exhales, lifting mood. Promising, not settled. At the bottom, mouth taping for better sleep: a handful of mixed studies, mostly in people with sleep apnea, and little agreement.

So the strongest case is for calm. That's useful: settling down before a test, a big moment or bed.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("The strongest evidence is for calm.", { y: 1.2, w: 9.5, h: 0.75, size: 38 });
        const rows = [
          ["Slow breathing calms heart rhythm", 1.0, "223 studies", "laborde2022"],
          ["Breathwork lowers stress", 0.66, "12 trials", "fincham2023"],
          ["Slow breathing before bed helps sleep", 0.3, "1 trial", "laborde2019"],
          ["Cyclic sighing lifts mood", 0.3, "1 trial", "balban2023"],
          ["Mouth taping helps sleep", 0.12, "9 mixed studies", "fangmeyer2025"],
        ];
        const x0 = 6.0, L = 4.9;
        rows.forEach(([t, v, n, ref], i) => {
          const y = 2.55 + i * 0.74;
          const top = i === 0;
          s.text(0.75, y - 0.22, 5.0, 0.45, t, { font: "light", size: 20, align: "right", color: top ? "ink" : "muted" });
          s.line(x0, y, x0 + v * L, y, { color: top ? "accent" : "muted", w: top ? 3 : 2 });
          s.circle(x0 + v * L, y, top ? 0.13 : 0.1, { fill: top ? "accent" : "muted" });
          s.text(x0 + v * L + 0.25, y - 0.2, 2.2, 0.4, n, { font: "cond", size: 17, color: top ? "accent" : "muted" });
          s.cite(ref);
        });
        s.line(x0, 2.2, x0, 5.75, { color: "rule", w: 1 });
        s.text(x0, 5.92, 6.0, 0.36, "Length: our reading of the evidence, not a score", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted" });
        s.source("Laborde 2022, 2019 · Fincham 2023 · Balban 2023 · Fangmeyer 2025", { x: 0.75, y: 6.2, w: 5.0, h: 0.36, refs: ["laborde2022", "fincham2023", "laborde2019", "balban2023", "fangmeyer2025"] });
        s.cite("hajghanbari2013");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 60,
      notes: `So here is the Monday version. Teach one slow-breathing drill, and teach it as a way to settle down, not as a way to go faster.

Slow, easy breaths with long exhales, for a few minutes. Before bed, before a test, before a big moment in competition, after a hard conversation. It costs nothing, and the side effects are close to none.

What we shouldn't do is sell it as a performance booster or an immune fix, because that isn't what the evidence shows. And anything involving rapid over-breathing or breath holds stays well away from water. If an athlete is using breathing to cope with anxiety that isn't going away, that is a conversation for Baylor's licensed mental health staff.`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(0.75, 1.55, 10.8, 2.0, "Teach one slow-breathing drill for settling down, not for speed.", { font: "condBold", size: 40, lineH: 1.05 });
        s.lead("Slow breaths, long exhales, a few minutes: before bed, a test or a big moment.", { y: 3.75, w: 9.5, h: 1.0, size: 24 });
        s.text(0.75, 4.85, 9, 0.36, "Example cues written for this deck.", { font: "light", size: 13, color: "muted" });
        s.source("Laborde et al. 2022 · Laborde et al. 2019", { y: 5.95, w: 8, refs: ["laborde2022", "laborde2019"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The line to take away: breathe slow for calm, and ask for proof on the rest.

Slow breathing is cheap, safe and backed by a lot of research for settling the body. The bigger promises, better immunity, better performance, fixing sleep with tape, mostly rest on one or two small studies, or none.

Question for the room: which breathing claims are already going around our locker rooms? Athletes hear these from podcasts and teammates long before they hear them from us. Knowing which ones are out there tells us where to start.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Breathe slow for calm. Ask for proof on the rest.", { y: 1.3, w: 7.8, h: 2.4, size: 56 });
        s.text(5.6, 4.35, 6.98, 1.6, "Which breathing claims are already going around our locker rooms?", { font: "medium", size: 30, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15, align: "right", valign: "bottom" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

