// 39 · Social Media and Athlete Mental Health: small on average, but how athletes use it matters more than how long.
// Composition: hook = small figure bottom-right in a large empty field, one-line headline
// top-left; takeaway = line centered with the question beneath; alignment = left. Problem =
// full-height soft panel on the left third; application = earned numbered steps (notice, ask, connect).

module.exports = {
  num: 39,
  slug: "social-media-and-athlete-mental-health",
  title: "Social Media and Athlete Mental Health",
  category: "Emerging / Wildcard Topics",
  palette: "slate",
  hero: { layout: "dose-response-curve", concept: "A gentle curve of risk against daily hours, flat through moderate use and rising past about three hours, with what the evidence can and can't say set beside it" },
  compose: { hook: "small-figure-field", takeaway: "centered", align: "left" },
  oneIdea: "On average the link between screen time and well-being is small; heavy use and how athletes use social media (comparison, passive scrolling, abuse) matter more than minutes. Our job is to notice, ask and connect athletes to licensed staff.",
  question: "Who does an athlete tell when the comments get personal?",
  caveats: `This deck is awareness, not training. It does not qualify anyone to assess or treat mental health, and it is not a substitute for care; anything clinical goes to Baylor's licensed mental health staff. Follow safe-messaging practice: no details of self-harm, no dramatic stories, always end on help-seeking. If anyone is in immediate danger, call 911; the 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline (call or text 988) is available any time. Confirm Baylor's own referral path and contacts before presenting. On the evidence: the 0.4% figure (Orben & Przybylski 2019) covers broad digital technology use in adolescents, from correlational data; other researchers argue effects are larger for heavy users and some subgroups, and the debate is live. The 3-hour finding (Riehm et al. 2019) is from US adolescents aged 12-15 and is an association ("internalizing problems" means anxiety and depression symptoms), not proof of cause. The athlete review (Siaj et al. 2026) found only 11 studies, mostly cross-sectional, one longitudinal; causation cannot be inferred and some studies found benefits (lower loneliness during COVID). The curve on slide 3 is an illustrative shape, not a plot of any one study.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "orben2019", cite: "Orben A, Przybylski AK. The association between adolescent well-being and digital technology use. Nat Hum Behav. 2019;3(2):173-182. doi:10.1038/s41562-018-0506-1", grade: "specification curve analysis of three large datasets (n = 355,358)", found: "external", supports: "The association between digital technology use and adolescent well-being is negative but small, explaining at most 0.4% of the variation." },
    { id: "riehm2019", cite: "Riehm KE, Feder KA, Tormohlen KN, et al. Associations between time spent using social media and internalizing and externalizing problems among US youth. JAMA Psychiatry. 2019;76(12):1266-1273. doi:10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2019.2325", grade: "longitudinal cohort (6,595 adolescents)", found: "external", supports: "After adjustment, more than 3 hours a day on social media was associated with higher risk of internalizing problems (anxiety and depression symptoms)." },
    { id: "przybylski2017", cite: "Przybylski AK, Weinstein N. A large-scale test of the Goldilocks hypothesis: quantifying the relations between digital-screen use and the mental well-being of adolescents. Psychol Sci. 2017;28(2):204-215. doi:10.1177/0956797616678438", grade: "preregistered cross-sectional analysis (n = 120,115)", found: "external", supports: "Links between screen time and well-being are curved; moderate use is not intrinsically harmful." },
    { id: "siaj2026", cite: "Siaj J, Domínguez-Balmaseda D, Vos-Saz A, Bonilla I. The impact of social media on athletes' mental health: a systematic review. Front Sports Act Living. 2026;8:1876771. doi:10.3389/fspor.2026.1876771", grade: "systematic review (11 studies, mostly cross-sectional)", found: "external", supports: "Passive engagement, appearance-based comparison and problematic use were most consistently linked to poorer outcomes; how athletes use social media may matter more than how much; causation cannot be inferred." },
    { id: "barry2024", cite: "Barry CT, Moran-Miller K, Levy HF, Gray T. Social media engagement, perceptions of social media costs and benefits, and well-being in college student-athletes. J Am Coll Health. 2024;72(8):2938-2947. doi:10.1080/07448481.2022.2142797", grade: "cross-sectional survey (1,120 US college athletes)", found: "external (findings as summarized in Siaj et al. 2026)", supports: "Higher social media engagement and perceived interference were associated with poorer well-being, including depression, anxiety and sleep." },
    { id: "houltberg2020", cite: "Houltberg BJ, Scholefield RM. Developmental model of elite athletes: the integration of developmental science and practitioner experience. Prof Psychol Res Pract. 2020;51(6):550-559. doi:10.1037/pro0000316", grade: "conceptual model from developmental science and practice", found: "Zotero", supports: "Social comparison now extends far beyond an athlete's peer circle through social media; public attention intensifies transitions for elite athletes (pp. 5-6 of the PDF)." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `Let's start with a number that surprises people. Researchers took three very large datasets, over three hundred and fifty thousand teenagers, and asked how much of the variation in their well-being lined up with their use of digital technology. The answer: at most about four tenths of one percent.

On average, that is small. But averages can hide a lot, and our athletes are not average social media users. Today is about what we know, what we don't, and what each of us can do.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Screen time’s share of teen well-being, at most:", { y: 1.25, w: 11.5, h: 0.85, size: 44 });
        s.figure("0.4%", { x: 7.2, y: 3.55, w: 5.38, h: 2.0, size: 150, align: "right", refs: ["orben2019"] });
        s.source("Orben & Przybylski, Nature Human Behaviour, 2019", { x: 6.5, y: 5.9, w: 6.08, align: "right", refs: ["orben2019"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `Here is what the average hides. In a national study of US teenagers, those on social media more than three hours a day were more likely to report symptoms of anxiety and depression a year later. That is an association, not proof that the apps caused it.

And for athletes, the minutes may matter less than what happens in them. A new review of athlete studies found the most consistent links were with comparing yourself to others, especially on appearance, and with passive scrolling. Our athletes live in public: their performances get judged online, by strangers, within minutes.

The honest caveat: there are only eleven athlete studies, almost all snapshots in time.`,
      build(s) {
        s.rect(0, 0, 4.443, 7.5, { fill: "soft" });
        s.label("On average", { x: 0.75, y: 1.75, w: 3.2 });
        s.text(0.75, 2.15, 3.3, 1.2, "Small.", { font: "condBold", size: 64, lineH: 1.0 });
        s.headline("The average hides heavy use and hard moments.", { x: 5.0, y: 1.3, w: 7.58, h: 1.7, size: 44 });
        s.text(5.0, 3.35, 7.5, 1.0, "Teens on social media over 3 hours a day: more anxiety and depression symptoms.", { font: "light", size: 22, lineH: 1.2 });
        s.text(5.0, 4.55, 7.5, 1.0, "Athletes: comparison and passive scrolling track with distress more than minutes do.", { font: "light", size: 22, lineH: 1.2 });
        s.source("Riehm et al. 2019 · Siaj et al. 2026", { x: 5.0, y: 5.9, w: 7.5, refs: ["riehm2019", "siaj2026"] });
        s.cite("houltberg2020");
      },
    },
    {
      role: "insight", time: 70,
      notes: `Put it together and you get a curve like this. Moderate use looks mostly fine; a very large study of English teenagers found moderate screen use was not harmful in itself. Risk climbs with heavy use, past about three hours a day.

But read the right side carefully. What the evidence can say: heavy use and harsh experiences online go along with more distress. What it can't say yet: that the feed caused it. Athletes who are already struggling may scroll more, not the other way round. It probably runs both ways.

So the useful question is less "how many hours?" and more "what is happening in those hours?"`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Risk climbs with heavy use. The cause is still unproven.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.0, h: 1.9, size: 56 });
        const x0 = 0.95, x1 = 7.3, yb = 5.65, H = 1.75;
        const X = h => x0 + h / 6.5 * (x1 - x0);
        s.line(x0, yb, x1, yb, { color: "muted", w: 1.25 });
        s.line(x0, yb, x0, yb - H, { color: "muted", w: 1.25 });
        const pts = [];
        for (let h = 0; h <= 6.5; h += 0.25) pts.push([X(h), yb - 0.35 - H * 0.72 * Math.pow(Math.max(0, h - 1.2) / 5.3, 2)]);
        s.path(pts, { smooth: true, color: "accent", w: 4.5 });
        s.line(X(3), yb, X(3), yb - H, { color: "rule", w: 1, dash: true });
        s.label("Daily hours", { x: x0, y: yb + 0.12, w: 2.5, size: 14 });
        s.label("3 h", { x: X(3) - 0.5, y: yb + 0.12, w: 1, size: 14, align: "center" });
        s.label("Risk", { x: x0 + 0.12, y: yb - H - 0.05, w: 1.5, size: 14 });
        s.label("Illustrative shape, not data", { x: x1 - 3.2, y: yb + 0.12, w: 3.2, size: 13, align: "right" });
        s.label("Can say", { x: 8.2, y: 3.85, w: 4.3 });
        s.text(8.2, 4.2, 4.38, 0.75, "Heavy use goes with more distress.", { font: "light", size: 20, lineH: 1.15 });
        s.label("Can’t say yet", { x: 8.2, y: 5.0, w: 4.3 });
        s.text(8.2, 5.35, 4.38, 0.75, "That the feed caused it.", { font: "light", size: 20, lineH: 1.15 });
        s.source("Przybylski & Weinstein 2017 · Riehm et al. 2019 · Siaj et al. 2026", { x: 5.0, y: 6.2, w: 7.58, align: "right", refs: ["przybylski2017", "riehm2019", "siaj2026"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "application", time: 65,
      notes: `So what is our part? Not diagnosis and not treatment. That belongs to Baylor's licensed mental health staff. Our part is three steps, in order.

First, notice. After a loss, a bad game or a moment that goes viral, watch for changes: sleep, mood, pulling away from teammates.

Second, ask, privately and plainly: "How has it been online since Saturday?" You don't need the perfect words. Asking does not make things worse.

Third, connect them to Baylor's licensed mental health staff, and help with the first step if they want it. If anyone is ever in immediate danger, call 911. The 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline is there any time, by call or text.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Our job is to notice and connect, not to treat.", { y: 1.2, w: 11.5, h: 0.9, size: 44 });
        const steps = [
          ["1", "Notice changes after a loss or a viral moment: sleep, mood, pulling away."],
          ["2", "Ask privately: “How has it been online?”"],
          ["3", "Connect them with Baylor’s licensed mental health staff."],
        ];
        steps.forEach(([k, t], i) => {
          const y = 2.65 + i * 1.12;
          s.text(0.75, y, 0.7, 0.7, k, { font: "cond", size: 36, color: "muted", lineH: 0.9 });
          s.text(1.6, y + 0.02, 10.9, 0.95, t, { font: i === 2 ? "medium" : "light", size: 26, lineH: 1.12, color: i === 2 ? "accent" : "ink" });
        });
        s.text(1.6, 6.0, 10.9, 0.36, "In an emergency, call 911. The 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline is available any time.", { font: "light", size: 14, color: "muted" });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "takeaway", time: 45,
      notes: `The line to take away: ask about the feed, not just the hours. The minutes matter less than what happens in them: comparison, criticism, abuse after a loss.

And the question for the room: who does an athlete tell when the comments get personal? A coach, an athletic trainer, a teammate, nobody? If we are not sure, that is worth fixing, and Baylor's mental health staff are the people to help us build that path.`,
      build(s) {
        s.headline("Ask about the feed, not just the hours.", { x: 0.75, y: 1.8, w: 11.83, h: 1.0, size: 60, align: "center" });
        s.line(6.17, 3.4, 7.17, 3.4, { color: "rule", w: 1.5 });
        s.text(1.5, 3.85, 10.33, 1.3, "Who does an athlete tell when the comments get personal?", { font: "medium", size: 34, color: "accent", lineH: 1.15, align: "center" });
      },
    },
  ],
};

