// 40 · AI in Sport Science: useful where its answers can be checked, unproven where they can't.
// Composition: hook = a two-line quote set right with its source under it; takeaway = two
// columns split by a vertical rule (line | question); alignment = right (right-aligned
// headlines, hero flush right). Problem = bare type set right; application = before/after
// with the "after" on a full-height soft panel.

module.exports = {
  num: 40,
  slug: "ai-in-sport-science",
  title: "AI in Sport Science",
  category: "Emerging / Wildcard Topics",
  palette: "paper",
  hero: { layout: "sort-two-columns", concept: "Four common uses sorted into 'useful now' and 'not there yet', each with the check that puts it there: estimates checked against real lifts and lab capture vs injury predictions never tested on new athletes" },
  compose: { hook: "quote-right", takeaway: "split-columns", align: "right" },
  oneIdea: "AI is useful where its answers can be checked against something real, and unproven where they can't. Ask what any model was checked against before acting on it.",
  question: "Which number on our screens have we never checked against what actually happened?",
  caveats: `Avoid both hype and dismissal. The Bullock et al. (2022) review covers musculoskeletal injury prediction models published to June 2021, both regression and machine learning; it says nothing about every AI tool, and newer models may be better. It found no external validation and 98% of models at high or unclear risk of bias. Claudino et al. (2019) found 58 team-sport AI studies with good reported performance but called for prospective evaluation. The 1RM study (Balsalobre-Fernández & Kipp 2021) is a lab study of the bench press on a Smith machine; errors of about 2.7-3.3 kg applied across methods, and plain regression did as well as the neural network, which is a useful point in itself: the "AI" step isn't always what helps. The 5-10 degree figure for markerless motion capture is from a 2026 position statement and depends on the joint and plane. Don't name vendors. If asked about large language models (chatbots): useful for drafting and summarizing when a person checks the output; the same "checked against what?" rule applies.`,
  sources: [
    { id: "bullock2022review", cite: "Bullock GS, Mylott J, Hughes T, Nicholson KF, Riley RD, Collins GS. Just how confident can we be in predicting sports injuries? A systematic review of the methodological conduct and performance of existing musculoskeletal injury prediction models in sport. Sports Med. 2022;52(10):2469-2482. doi:10.1007/s40279-022-01698-9", grade: "systematic review (30 studies, 204 models)", found: "Zotero", supports: "No study externally validated a model; 98% of models were at high or unclear risk of bias; 'No models could be recommended for use in practice.'" },
    { id: "bullock2022blackbox", cite: "Bullock GS, Hughes T, Arundale AH, Ward P, Collins GS, Kluzek S. Black box prediction methods in sports medicine deserve a red card for reckless practice: a change of tactics is needed to advance athlete care. Sports Med. 2022;52(8):1729-1735. doi:10.1007/s40279-022-01655-6", grade: "current opinion", found: "Zotero", supports: "Proprietary and machine learning models are often opaque (no code or equations) and not externally validated, so their performance can't be checked." },
    { id: "claudino2019", cite: "Claudino JG, Capanema DO, de Souza TV, Serrão JC, Machado Pereira AC, Nassis GP. Current approaches to the use of artificial intelligence for injury risk assessment and performance prediction in team sports: a systematic review. Sports Med Open. 2019;5(1):28. doi:10.1186/s40798-019-0202-3", grade: "systematic review (58 studies)", found: "external", supports: "AI is widely applied in team sports with good reported metrics; prospective evaluation is needed to establish predictive performance." },
    { id: "balsalobre2021", cite: "Balsalobre-Fernández C, Kipp K. Use of machine-learning and load-velocity profiling to estimate 1-repetition maximums for two variations of the bench-press exercise. Sports (Basel). 2021;9(3):39. doi:10.3390/sports9030039", grade: "single lab study", found: "Zotero", supports: "Bench-press 1RM estimated from submaximal load-velocity data with mean errors of 2.7-3.3 kg across five methods, including a neural network; regression did best." },
    { id: "collings2026", cite: "Collings TJ, Diamond LE, Barrett RS, et al. An evidence-based and mechanistic approach to reducing the risk of anterior cruciate ligament injury: an Exercise and Sport Science Australia position statement. Sports Med. 2026;56(10):2389-2411. doi:10.1007/s40279-026-02450-3", grade: "position statement", found: "Zotero", supports: "Markerless pose estimation can record motion to within about 5-10 degrees of marker-based motion capture, depending on joint and plane (p. 9 of the PDF)." },
    { id: "reis2024", cite: "Reis FJJ, Alaiti RK, Vallio CS, Hespanhol L. Artificial intelligence and machine learning approaches in sports: concepts, applications, challenges, and future perspectives. Braz J Phys Ther. 2024;28(3):101083. doi:10.1016/j.bjpt.2024.101083", grade: "narrative review", found: "external", supports: "AI and ML show promise for injury prediction, performance analysis and personalized training; challenges include data quality, interpretability and ethics." },
  ],
  slides: [
    {
      role: "hook", time: 45,
      notes: `AI is being sold into every corner of sport, and injury prediction is the big promise: a system that tells you which athlete is about to get hurt.

So researchers went and looked. They reviewed every published injury prediction model they could find in sport, two hundred and four of them, some built with machine learning, some with older statistics. Their conclusion is on the screen: no models could be recommended for use in practice.

That is not the end of the AI story. It's a good place to start sorting it out.`,
      build(s) {
        s.text(3.4, 1.9, 9.18, 2.6, "“No models could be recommended for use in practice.”", { font: "condBold", size: 60, lineH: 1.02, color: "accent", align: "right", role: "headline" });
        s.lead("Reviewers of 204 sports injury prediction models", { x: 5.6, y: 4.75, w: 6.98, h: 0.5, size: 22, align: "right" });
        s.source("Bullock et al., Sports Medicine, 2022", { x: 6.5, y: 5.35, w: 6.08, h: 0.36, align: "right", valign: "top", refs: ["bullock2022review"] });
      },
    },
    {
      role: "problem", time: 60,
      notes: `The problem is not that the models are bad at math. It's that almost nobody checked them properly.

In that review, not one of the two hundred and four models had been tested on a new group of athletes, which is the only test that tells you whether it works on your team. Nearly all were at high or unclear risk of bias. An earlier review of AI in team sports found fifty-eight studies reporting good accuracy, and still called for proper testing going forward.

And many commercial scores are black boxes. The company does not show how the number is made, so nobody outside can check it.`,
